`routed` is 1 after the 20.9 throw — because route() increments routed BEFORE rightNetwork.send throws. The test asserts the throw precedes the hop. Which behavior is right? "any cross-subnet frame takes exactly one router hop, incrementing routed" — the frame DID take a router hop (attempted); it died at the destination. Arguably the counter should count routed frames that actually crossed. The DL-1 fail-loud: a throw means nothing was delivered. I'll move the increment after a successful send... but then lastFrame set after too. Reorder in router.route:

```js
function route({ from, to, request }) {
  if (!rightNetwork) throw ...
  const record = rightNetwork.send({ from, to, request });
  routed += 1;
  lastFrame = ...;
  return {...record, viaRouter: leftAddress};
}
```

Now the hop only counts when the frame actually crossed. Update router.js.

